                decoder -- THE PROJECT GLOSSARY AS KANT DATA.

    Pure data, no actions (the jigcorpus/unitTests pattern). Included by
    `incant/decode` (the verb) and `incant/decodeT` (the POP). NO Start()/stop()
    wrapper -- this is a registry file, not an execution file.

    CANONICAL: this is the source-of-truth term corpus. Brief: docs/decoder.md.
    Seeded 2026-08-09 from the decoder dispatch (Clay via Tony), transcribed by
    Clod per WT-9.

    ============================================================================
    THE ONE RULE THAT SHAPES EVERY ENTRY: PRESENT-TENSE ONLY, ONE SENTENCE.
    Where a term drifted across seals, the entry records the CURRENT BINDING and
    nothing else. History stays in the seals. The decoder is a lookup table with
    a print verb -- not a runner, not vigram infrastructure, not documentation.

    ⚠ TRANSCRIPTION PROVENANCE IS PART OF EACH ENTRY, because this artifact
    exists to kill citation rot and would be absurd if its own entries were
    cited rather than measured. Two classes, per the dispatch:

      DICTATED  the dispatch's sentence is the binding, transcribed as given.
      PULLED    the dispatch marked it a citation, not a measurement, so the
                binding sentence was read from the MINTING SEAL and the seal
                wins where the two disagree.

    THREE PULLS CAME BACK CORRECTED, and they are the artifact justifying
    itself on its first run:
      H4    the dispatch's sentence is rung 1's DISCHARGE of H4, not H4.
      H9    the dispatch's held sentence is the COROLLARY; the primary is the
            idiom-family rule.
      degradeAssertsOccurrence  materially different -- the seal is about
            fallback SOUNDNESS, the draft was about jit OCCURRENCE.
    ============================================================================

    ⚠ `parked` IS AN UNRATIFIED SLOT, NOT AN ENTRY. Held by Clay 2026-08-09 on
    a collision he found in his own draft: the dispatch's candidate and SEQ 44's
    PINCH 6 draw the line differently, and PINCH 6 was written first. Both
    sentences sit side by side below until Tony rules. See docs/decoder.md.
*****************************************************************************/
